    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83954.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Sure. Well, in the book, I talk about why we have a District of Columbia, and it really started for a few reasons. But the primary one was the capital and the seat of the federal government was in Philadelphia. And there were a group of revolutionary war veterans who didn't feel that they were getting their compensation, their veterans benefits at the time. And so they closed down Congress. They just shut down the ability of Congress to meet. And because of the politics, the state of Pennsylvania did nothing to help in that situation. And so George Washington and James Madison and Thomas Jefferson and John Adams got together for dinner and they talked about we need a separate piece of land that is the federal government's land, not the state's land. And this was really something that was contrary to the Declaration of Independence and what the Constitution would talk about later, because it really was a recognition at the time that states had given up their power to create the federal government. It wasn't the federal government that created subservient political jurisdictions. And so the four of them met and they decided we're going to have a district of Colombia, we're gonna have a federal district And then they started talking about where should it be located. And James Madison wanted it located in the north near the monetary banks and those that control the money. And Thomas Jefferson and George Washington wanted it located south where it would be more centrally located and wouldn't, the slave issue was an issue at the time. And so they chose this a piece of Maryland and Virginia as the District of Columbia. And it was the out the map for the district the plan for the district was created by Pierre LaFon and the district then grew really into what it is today. But it was always recognized that Congress the legislative branch the people's house would sit on a hill, and the rest of the federal district would look up to Article 1. And again, it was significant that Congress was Article 1. It was the most important branch because it was the first branch mentioned in the Constitution. And all of those things talk about legislative supremacy, and we've lost legislative supremacy in this country. We now have executive branch supremacy, and we have bureaucrats making decisions that members of Congress, United States senators, should be making. And unfortunately, we have veered away. But absolutely, when we talk about the DC statehood bill that was passed recently in the US House, it is absolutely contrary to what our founders envisioned and would be very, very dangerous to the idea of having an independent federal government.",
