  {
    "episode_id": 83954,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83954.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, absolutely right. We are supposed to have. And now, again, you hear from the liberals, the radicals that We have three co-equal branches. We never intended, the founders never intended to have three co-equal branches. The judiciary was never a co-equal branch. The judiciary was designed to be a branch that would settle differences between the other two branches that would act as a referee, but it was never designed as a super legislature. It was never designed as a super executive branch, and it has become that and has become that. And I write about it in the book, one of the reasons why Supreme Court nominations are so controversial is because the left believes that they can use the Supreme Court to override constitutional provisions, and it's unfortunate. But the legislative branch was intended to be the branch that was closest to the people. If you look at the design of Washington, D.C., and I take my guess out to the speaker's balcony, and you can look out at the Washington Monument and the Lincoln Memorial on the mall, and the mall was a public space that was designed to allow the American public to redress grievances with the people's house and allow people to gather and so that they could protest against their government. Hopefully sometimes compliment those in government service, but certainly the design of Washington DC was meant to recognize the prominence of the legislative branch.",
