    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely, and it's an interesting story because he was very important in the revolutionary, Lan Phan was very important in the revolutionary effort to rid ourselves of the British monarchy control. And so George Washington rewarded him by giving him this position of architect at the Catholic. And the what he what he did was there was a house that was in the path of a road that he wanted that La Falle wanted to build. And so he asked the owner, you know, can we buy this house? And the owner said, no, this is my house and I don't want to sell this house. And so La Falle had troops go and tear down the house and and and destroy this man's private property. And when The owner actually, his brother was a friend of George Washington. There's a connection, interesting connection that I talked about in the book to Georgetown University and other things that happened in Washington, DC. But the owner of the house led George Washington to know what happened. And George Washington convened a meeting with James Madison and John Adams and Thomas Jefferson again. And Thomas Jefferson drafted the letter to the owner of the Harold's, Mr. Carroll. apologizing for its destruction and also a letter to La Fone explaining why he was going to be fired. But somebody from France would not understand the significance of private property that's embedded in our Declaration of Independence, embedded in our United States Constitution, and embedded in the ideals that the founders held and wanted us to continue in this country.",
