  {
    "episode_id": 83954,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83954.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "Colleen, one of the amazing things about private property was it just isn't a house. It's not your car. It's not the furniture. It's actually your thoughts. And it's your beliefs. It's your religious beliefs. It's your political beliefs. we have ownership of those things, and was always intended to have ownership. And that's why it's so important that we recognize that our rights are given to us by God. They're not given to us by the government, because then they can be taken away by government. They can be infringed on by government. But how dare government try to take something away that God has given us? And the founders believe that with all of their core, they could have been big government founders like John Adams, or they could have been small government founders like Thomas Jefferson, didn't matter. They believed that property was so vital in how we developed as a country and they saw property in a very, very broad way.",
