  {
    "episode_id": 83954,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83954.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Absolutely. I think it's fascinating. Not just that George Washington did that, but that the reaction in Europe to what George Washington did, all these people in Europe had sought power, Napoleon and other leaders in Europe, they continued to try to build empires in Europe, and they tried to continue to sort of build their own status. And here was a man who was humble enough to say, my country is more important than me. What I do in this instance, history will take note of. And so he resigned his commission as the most powerful and popular man in America. He resigned his commission And he did it to Congress. He didn't do it to the president. He did it to Congress. He said, this is so important that I'm going to go before the Continental Congress and I'm going to resign my commission. And there's a large painting in Congress in statutory hall showing George Washington resigning his commission. And it's all about the fact that we are a civilian government and the civilian government receives its power from the people. It does not receive its power from other government officials or take power away from the people, but it actually receives power from the people.",
