  {
    "episode_id": 83954,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83954.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "And it's a history that we should celebrate and really that the rest of the world celebrates at the time when we have radicals tearing down our history, the rest of the world is looking at us puzzled. thinking, wait a second, this is a country that really has benefited from recognizing the faults of its history and moving forward. But it's a great story. And it's one of many great stories, I think, in the book. But Philip Reed was a slave. He was purchased in South Carolina. And he was brought to Washington, D.C. And he worked in a statue making facility in a foundry. And that foundry was responsible for and received the contract for the statue of freedom. which is on the dome of the United States Capitol. And Philip Reed, a very bright, not educated, but a very bright man, was able to figure out how to take the plaster mold from Italy that was brought to the United States, make this beautiful statue of freedom, and put it on top of the dome. And in 1863, he was given his freedom from slavery for during the Emancipation Proclamation of the District of Columbia, not the 1864, more famous Emancipation Proclamation, but the 1863 Emancipation Proclamation of the District of Columbia. So he was a free man at the time, still working for the same company, the same business, and was responsible for putting that statute of freedom. And I think to myself, What a great, how he must have just overflowed with pride knowing that at one point in time he was a slave and then he erected a statue that all Americans recognized, that all Americans celebrate. And again, it was just a huge step in progress in terms of where America was and where in the path that it started down.",
