  {
    "episode_id": 83954,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83954.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 32,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, I think that's how we grow as a country. And so if we look at our country, there have been presidents, President Adams, the second president of the United States, John Adams, and also President Woodrow Wilson, who enacted laws or had their allies in Congress enact laws and then implemented those laws, the Sedition Act that would punish people in the United States who disagreed with the government and who spoke ill of the government. That's what we're about. You know, the protests that are going on now, there's nothing wrong with the protest. There's something wrong with vandalizing statues, there's something wrong with looting stores, but there's nothing wrong with protesting. We want people to feel comfortable protesting. We want to be uncomfortable with issues that other people raise so that they challenge the way we think and they make us better as a country. And that's really what our freedoms are based on. No other country in the world would put up with this. In fact, I tell a joke in one of the chapters about the President Reagan told And he said, you know, there was an American talking to a Russian. And the American said, you know, what's great about America is I can stand up and say that President Reagan is, you know, a lousy president. And the Russian said, well, wait a second, that's no different. In Russia, I can stand up and say that President Reagan is a lousy president also. And the point is that there's only some things you can say in Russia. There's only some things you can say in China. There's only some things you can say in many African or Asian countries. But in America, you can speak your mind. And as long as you don't incite violence, as long as you don't create the kind of conditions that would hurt other people, if you're just challenging them intellectually, we welcome that in America. We may disagree. We may turn off the TV. We may walk away from a protest. That's okay. But it's not okay to silence someone from speaking their mind. And it's also not okay to make sure that we don't feel the ability to speak our own mind because it's part of the obligation we have as Americans.",
